What it does (every hunk is marked [rexglue-native]):
- The setting native_render_targets ("GPU", bool, default false, kRequiresRestart) is defined in
  vulkan/render_target_cache.cpp. It is read once, in VulkanRenderTargetCache::Initialize after
  InitializeCommon, and only takes effect on the host render-target path. It is read at start, not
  live, because render targets made without transfers hold nothing the EDRAM emulation could carry
  on from, and the other way round; the initial clears also only apply to targets created while
  it's on. The game sets it before the GPU starts, from cod3_renderer native.
  The base class gets IsNativeRenderTargets() (public) and SetNativeRenderTargets() (protected).
  D3D12 never sets it.
- RenderTargetCache::Update: in native mode the transfer lists are dropped, at 0300's
  native_census_skip_transfers place. Ownership is still recorded, and the census still counts
  the transfers (as "dropped").
- PrepareHostRenderTargetsResolveClear: in native mode the transfers are dropped after
  ChangeOwnership. Choice: only the render targets the resolve names are cleared (in the cleared
  rectangle), not every image whose recorded range overlaps the cleared tiles. N1 counted 0
  resolve clears in CoD3, so the simplest correct form was kept; overlap clearing can come later
  if a game needs it.
- RenderTargetCache::ClearCache: returns at once in native mode (a native target owns its
  contents, not tiles). VulkanRenderTargetCache::ClearCache still destroys framebuffers and
  render passes first.
- A new Vulkan render target in native mode starts cleared: color 0, depth 1.0, stencil 0.
  CreateRenderTarget queues it in native_initial_clears_. PerformNativeInitialClears clears each
  queued target as a whole, with the existing resolve-clear machinery:
  PerformTransfersAndResolveClears(1, &rt, no transfers, guest clear value, whole rectangle).
  The guest depth 1.0 is xenos::Float32To20e4(1.0f) << 8 for D24FS8 (0.5 on the host, as resolve
  clears convert it) and 0xFFFFFF << 8 for D24S8. It is called in VulkanRenderTargetCache::Update
  before the transfers, and in Resolve right after PrepareHostRenderTargetsResolveClear (which may
  create targets). It is not done inside CreateRenderTarget, because base Update is still binding
  the draw's targets there. Shutdown empties the queue before DestroyAllRenderTargets.
  No new Vulkan function or deferred command was needed: vkCmdClearDepthStencilImage isn't in
  device_1_0.inc, and adding it would change the layout of VulkanDevice::Functions.

kEdramBaseTilesBits (11) and 0010's 4096-tile EDRAM: no fix needed.
  RB_COLOR_INFO.color_base and RB_DEPTH_INFO.depth_base are 11-bit fields in registers.h (bit 11 is
  a separate field that nothing reads). So every base is 0..2047 before it reaches a
  RenderTargetKey, and the key's 11-bit base_tiles masks nothing. 0010's extra tiles are reached
  by surfaces extending past tile 2047 (ownership ranges up to 4096), and by resolve areas past
  it (ResolveEdramInfo base_tiles_bit_11), not by bases. CoD3 at 1920x1080 uses 3264 tiles with
  both bases below 2048. A guest base with bit 11 set would wrap mod 2048 in both draws and
  resolves alike, as on the console. Harmless today, and nothing to change for native mode.

